extract_dedup.log — CDS extraction & deduplication, old vs. nf reference
==========================================================================
Generated: 2026-07-08

Purpose
-------
The old pipeline uses one dedup-CDS reference (genome-wide, protein-coding
only) for both v2 and v7/v8_b. The nf pipeline splits this into TWO separate
references instead: a genome-wide one (v7_nf/v8_b_nf, for a fair like-for-like
comparison against old v7/v8_b) and a smaller MANE_Select-restricted one
(v2_nf, since v2_nf was deliberately scoped differently from what v2 actually
used — see Map_scripts/README3.md). This log records how each of the three
was built and their resulting sequence counts, for direct comparison.

Old reference (NCBI RefSeq GRCh38.p14)
---------------------------------------
Source        : GCF_000001405.40_GRCh38.p14_cds_from_genomic.fna
                (downloaded pre-spliced/pre-oriented directly from NCBI —
                no extraction step needed; NCBI already provides one FASTA
                entry per CDS, ID format "lcl|NC_..._cds_NP_..._N")
Dedup command : seqkit rmdup -s -i GCF_000001405.40_GRCh38.p14_cds_from_genomic.fna \
                    -o GCF_000001405.40_GRCh38.p14_cds_from_genomic.dedup.fna --threads 12
Dedup basis   : exact sequence match (-s flag)
Result        : 93,088 unique sequences retained; 53,249 exact duplicates removed
Pre-dedup     : 146,337 (93,088 + 53,249)
Known issue   : downstream v7 scripts originally grouped reads by
                cds_id.split('|')[0], which collapses every CDS to the
                literal string "lcl" because the ID itself contains '|'.
                Patched post-hoc for existing results by
                Map_scripts/mappability_v7_fix_uf.sh (re-run Step 3 only,
                using '|'.join(name.split('|')[:-1]) as the grouping key) —
                the v7 scripts themselves were never corrected at the source.

New reference — genome-wide (Ensembl GRCh38.116, nf, v7_nf/v8_b_nf)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Source        : Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.116.gtf + Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.dna_sm.primary_assembly.fa
                (nf-core/rnaseq-recommended pair; no pre-spliced CDS FASTA
                is distributed, so extraction had to be done from scratch)
Extraction    : build_dedup_cds_nf.py --scope genome-wide (standalone; same
                logic also embedded as Step 0d in
                Map_scripts/mappability_correction_v7_a_nf.sh for cluster runs)
  - Parsed all CDS-type feature lines in the GTF
  - Filtered to transcript_biotype "protein_coding" (matches the old
    reference's implicit protein-coding-only scope, since NCBI's
    cds_from_genomic.fna only ever contained CDS features)
  - Spliced multi-exon CDS per transcript via pysam.FastaFile (in-process
    fetch, not per-exon samtools subprocess calls — needed at this scale)
  - Reverse-complemented minus-strand transcripts
  - Header = plain versioned transcript ID (e.g. ENST00000256078.10) —
    deliberately no '|' characters, avoiding the old v7 ID-grouping bug
    class entirely rather than patching it after the fact
Dedup command : seqkit rmdup -s -i Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.dna_sm.primary_ass.dedup_cds_raw.fna \
                    -o Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.dna_sm.primary_ass.dedup_cds.fna --threads 4
Dedup basis   : exact sequence match (-s flag) — same criterion as old reference
Result        : 182,639 unique sequences retained; 95,845 exact duplicates removed
Pre-dedup     : 278,484 protein-coding transcripts with CDS (0 skipped during extraction)
Run location  : local (laptop), not the cluster — genome+GTF and seqkit were
                already available locally; result copied to
                Ref/Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.dna_sm.primary_ass.dedup_cds.fna for scp
                to the cluster's <HPC_WORKDIR>/mappability-correction/Ref/
                (nf reference files live inside Ref/ now, not a separate nfRef/)

New reference — MANE-restricted (Ensembl GRCh38.116, nf, v2_nf)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Source        : same Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.116.gtf + primary_assembly.fa pair
Extraction    : build_dedup_cds_nf.py --scope mane (same logic embedded as
                Step 0d in Map_scripts/mappability_correction_v2_a_nf.sh)
  - Pass 1: scanned all `transcript`-feature lines for tag "MANE_Select"
    → 19,299 MANE_Select transcripts found
  - Pass 2: parsed CDS-type feature lines, kept only those belonging to a
    MANE_Select transcript (not filtered by transcript_biotype, since
    MANE_Select membership already implies protein-coding)
  - Same splicing/revcomp/plain-ID approach as the genome-wide extraction
Dedup command : seqkit rmdup -s -i Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.dna_sm.primary_ass.dedup_cds_mane_raw.fna \
                    -o Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.dna_sm.primary_ass.dedup_cds_mane.fna --threads 4
Dedup basis   : exact sequence match (-s flag) — same criterion as both above
Result        : 19,132 unique sequences retained; 97 exact duplicates removed
Pre-dedup     : 19,229 transcripts with CDS (0 skipped during extraction)
Note          : 19,299 MANE_Select transcripts were tagged, but only 19,229
                have any CDS feature — the missing 70 are the same
                non-coding (RNU/SNORD/lncRNA) MANE_Select transcripts already
                identified in id_conversion_check_nf.tsv, consistent since
                ncRNAs have no CDS by definition
Run location  : local (laptop); result copied to
                Ref/Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.dna_sm.primary_ass.dedup_cds_mane.fna
                for scp to the cluster's Ref/

Comparison table
----------------
                                  Old (NCBI p14)   New genome-wide (nf)   New MANE-restricted (nf)
CDS feature lines scanned         n/a (pre-spliced)      2,717,081                191,809
Transcripts with CDS (pre-dedup)       146,337              278,484                 19,229
Avg. CDS segments/exons per tx             n/a                 9.76                   9.97
Exact duplicates removed                53,249               95,845                     97
Unique CDS sequences (post-dedup)       93,088              182,639                 19,132
Dedup fraction (removed/pre-dedup)        36.4%                34.4%                   0.5%
Extraction skipped/failed                    n/a                    0                      0

Interpretation
--------------
The nf/Ensembl genome-wide reference has ~1.9x as many pre-dedup
protein-coding transcripts (278,484 vs. 146,337) and ~2.0x as many unique CDS
sequences after dedup (182,639 vs. 93,088) as the old NCBI RefSeq reference.
This reflects Ensembl's more expansive isoform annotation (predicted/
non-canonical transcripts, alternative CDS starts, etc.) relative to NCBI's
more curated RefSeq transcript set — not a difference in dedup methodology,
since all three used the identical seqkit rmdup -s exact-match criterion.
The genome-wide dedup fraction is close between old and new (36.4% vs.
34.4%), suggesting a similar underlying rate of redundant CDS sequence per
transcript in both annotations, despite the near-doubling in transcript
count.

The MANE-restricted reference's dedup fraction (0.5%) is dramatically lower
than either genome-wide reference (~35%) — expected, since MANE_Select
deliberately selects one representative transcript per gene, so most
cross-transcript redundancy from alternative isoforms of the same gene is
already excluded before dedup even runs. The 97 duplicates that remain are
presumably genuinely distinct genes with identical CDS (e.g. recent
paralogs, or genes annotated twice under different IDs).

Average CDS segment (exon) count per transcript is essentially identical
between the genome-wide and MANE-restricted sets (9.76 vs. 9.97) — the
speed difference in extracting them is not because MANE transcripts splice
more simply, but because the splicing/fetching loop (the dominant cost)
scales with the number of retained transcripts: ~19k vs. ~278k, about 14x
fewer pysam.fetch() calls.

These size differences are a relevant finding for the reference comparison
this project is running: v7_nf/v8_b_nf reads will have roughly twice as much
competing CDS sequence to multimap against as the old v7/v8_b runs, while
v2_nf reads compete against a CDS pool two orders of magnitude smaller than
either genome-wide reference — independent of any genome-coordinate or
annotation-version effects already characterized in reference_differences.docx.
